his problem is harder than it looks because of the word distinct.

Example

For:

aaaa

Valid substrings are:

aa
aaa
aaaa

Output:

3

Even though "aa" occurs multiple times, it is counted once.

Why brute force won't work

There are:

O(n²)

substrings.

With |S| = 100000, generating all substrings is impossible.

Key Idea: Suffix Automaton (SAM)

A Suffix Automaton stores all distinct substrings of a string.

Instead of enumerating substrings, we count only the distinct ones represented in the automaton.

Observation

We need distinct substrings such that:

Length ≥ 2
First character = Last character

Suppose we are at the root of the SAM.

For every transition:

root --(c)--> state u

Any valid substring must:

start with c
end with c
have length ≥ 2

So after taking the first character c, we need a non-empty continuation from u whose last character is also c.

DP on SAM

Let:

dp[state][ch]

= number of distinct strings obtainable from state
that end with character ch.

Recurrence

For every transition:

state --(x)--> next
The one-character string "x" contributes:
dp[state][x] += 1
Any string from next ending with character c
can be prefixed with x:
dp[state][c] += dp[next][c]

Because the SAM is a DAG, this DP can be computed in decreasing order of state length.

Final Answer

For every root transition:

root --(c)--> u

count:

dp[u][c]

Why?

We already used the first character c.
dp[u][c] counts all non-empty suffixes ending in c.
Therefore:
c + suffix

starts with c, ends with c, and has length at least 2.

So:

answer = Σ dp[u][c]

over all root transitions.

Complexity
Building SAM: O(n)
DP: O(26 × number_of_states)
Number of states ≤ 2n

So:

Time:  O(26·n)
Space: O(26·n)

which works comfortably for n = 100000.


import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);

        String s = sc.next();

        // HashSet stores only unique substrings
        Set<String> set = new HashSet<>();

        int n = s.length();

        // Generate all possible substrings
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {

            // Start j from i+1 because length must be >= 2
            for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {

                // Check first and last character
                if (s.charAt(i) == s.charAt(j)) {

                    // Extract substring from i to j
                    String sub = s.substring(i, j + 1);

                    // HashSet automatically removes duplicates
                    set.add(sub);
                }
            }
        }

        System.out.println(set.size());
    }
}